1. **CRITICAL — the final annex falsely closes the central above-\(r_0\) box.**

> “The converse's UNPROVED status and W2-OPEN-2's above-r₀ chain claims are unchanged.”

> “W2-OPEN-2's residual open surface: the converse (recorded ξ ≠ 1 ⟹ byte-RED) only”

These statements are incompatible. The Σ-LAW annex closes only OPEN-2a’s shared-below forward implication. It does not prove W2-OPEN-2(a)–(b), namely polygon/valuation transfer and strict residual transport above the first twist. Indeed, the preceding annex explicitly says those claims remain unchanged. Moreover, the converse is a flagged premise condition, not W2-OPEN-2’s chain-level conclusion. Annex #3 therefore records an unsupported closure of the note’s principal open lemma.

2. **GAP — W2-C1 assumes a scalar orbit outside the only perimeter where scalarity is proved.**

> “Outside (C-coll) the law is still the exact recursion (HR-REC) … only the two-parameter SCALAR normal form below needs the perimeter.”

> “At a shared-key junction on the §3 perimeter, the print residual of the harness lift is  
> \(R_{r-1}(\Phi_{m+1}^{har})(y)=w_{m+1}\cdot\psi_m(\xi_{m+1}\cdot y)\).”

> “the exact law is (HR-REC), the scalar form is proved only on (C-coll). On the roster every junction is either on (C-coll) or monomial-collapsing with scalar orbit anyway”

The proof of W2-C1 says \(c/\rho_j=w\xi^j\), but §3.3(c) supplies only recursively computed \(\rho_j\), not their geometricity. The roster’s single non-collapse example having a scalar orbit is instance evidence, not a general theorem.

Counter-configuration criterion: take a legal non-collapse junction with \(0,1,2\in\operatorname{supp}\psi_m\). A scalar orbit requires
\[
\rho_1^2=\rho_0\rho_2.
\]
HR-REC plus noncommuting mod-wrap supplies no identity forcing this relation. Until such an identity is proved, W2-C1 is justified only at \(m=1\), on (C-coll), or on individually scanned scalar-orbit instances.

3. **GAP — W2-OPEN-2’s advertised “equivalent sufficient form” is degree-inconsistent with the note’s own index dictionary.**

> “GMN keys \(\phi_i\) = harness \(\Phi_{i-1}\)”

> “writing \(\Phi_q^{har}=\phi_q^{tw}+D_q\) with \(\deg D_q<\deg\phi_q\)”

These cannot hold generally. The dictionary pairs \(\phi_q^{tw}\) with \(\Phi_{q-1}^{har}\), not \(\Phi_q^{har}\). For any junction with \(e_{q-1}g_{q-1}>1\),
\[
\deg\Phi_q=e_{q-1}g_{q-1}\deg\Phi_{q-1}>\deg\phi_q,
\]
so \(\Phi_q-\phi_q\) retains the degree of \(\Phi_q\) and cannot have degree below \(\phi_q\). The earlier P3 repair corrected the premise but missed this second occurrence, leaving the proposed discharge route malformed.

4. **GAP — “same induced valuation data” contradicts the note’s own reason representative invariance is unavailable.**

> “equivalence as type data (same residual classes, same induced valuation data)”

> “the recorded data warrant only ≥ key weight — with equality generic … strictly BELOW γ, so the two chains' inductive valuations may genuinely differ as functions on \(O[x]\)”

The second quote directly denies the first if “induced valuation data” includes the augmented valuations. Counter-configuration: if \(\Phi=\phi+D\) with \(\mu(D)=\mu(\phi)<\gamma\), the \(\phi\)-augmentation gives
\[
\mu_\phi(\Phi)=\min(\gamma,\mu(D))=\mu(D)<\gamma,
\]
whereas the \(\Phi\)-augmentation assigns \(\Phi\) value \(\gamma\). Thus strict representatives of the same residual modulus need not induce the same valuation function. W2-C3 may claim matched residual/type ledger data at its fenced scope, but the stronger parenthetical is not established.

5. **GAP — PE5/PE6 do not certify the as-built annexed document.**

> “Target … byte-identical to r4 commit 94534df”

> “The note is ACCEPTED … PE5 … and PE6 … CLEAN”

Both clean passes examined the frozen post-r4 body. The acceptance record and subsequent OPEN2ATTACK/Σ-LAW annexes were appended afterward. Consequently, their clean verdicts provide no review coverage for the annex language—precisely where Finding 1 occurs. The annex mechanism may be legitimate ledger procedure, but it is not a two-clean certification of the present as-built text.

